Nope, it's not the same as using mean group size.

(In formulae:

\hat{N} = sum_{i=1}^n \frac{s_i}{\hat{p}(z_i)}

i.e., the sum of s_i/p(z_i), summed over all observed animals

is not the same as

\hat{N} = \frac{sum_{i=1}^n s_i}{n}  \frac{1}{\hat{p}(z_i)}

i.e., mean group size times sum of 1/p(z_i) summed over all observed animals

unless all of the z_i's are the same - i.e., no covariates.)

You can think of it as using an estimate of the population mean group
size, rather than the observed mean group size.

Best, Len

On 02/10/2019 00:53, Ana Cañadas wrote:
> Thanks Len!
> That helps!
> SO MRDS truly uses the mean group size to estimate abundance, it doesn't
> use any regression line for size bias. That is great news for me,
> because I wanted to use the mean and I had done everything previously in
> MRDS so I was afraid I had to redo everything  in MCDS, but it seems
> that I don't!
> I thought I would see you here this week!
> Cheers
> Ana
>
> El mar., 1 oct. 2019 a las 19:30, Len Thomas

> (<len.t...@st-andrews.ac.uk <mailto:len.t...@st-andrews.ac.uk>>)

> escribió:
>
>     Hi Ana, (list)
>
>     Summary: expected cluster size is estimated number of individuals
>     divided by estimated number of clusters.
>
>     Longer answer - first some notation:
>     Let i = 1, ... , n index the observations; let z_i be the vector of
>     detection function covariates for observation i, which might include
>     s_i
>     the observed cluster size for that observation.  Assume we've fitted a
>     detection function using MCDS or MRDS and that \hat{p}(z_i) is the
>     estimated detection probability for the ith observation given its
>     covariates z_i.
>
>     Then, the estimated number of individuals in the covered area (i.e.,
>     the
>     transects) is given by the Horvitz-Thompson-like estimator
>
>     \hat{N} = sum_{i=1}^n \frac{s_i}{\hat{p}(z_i)}
>
>     (in case you're not used to LaTeX notation that's the sum over all
>     observations of s_i divided by \hat{p}(z_i)).
>
>     The estimated number of clusters is given by
>
>     \hat{N_c} = sum_{i=1}^n \frac{1}{\hat{p}(z_i)}
>
>     (similar but the sum of 1 divided by \hat{p}(z_i)).
>
>     The estimated expected cluster size is simply \hat{N_c} divided by
>     \hat{N}.
>
>     So, it's not really that the abundance estimates are calculated using
>     the expected cluster size - instead it's the expected cluster size is
>     calculated using the abundance estimates!
>
>     Hope this helps! Len
>
>     On 01/10/2019 23:48, Ana Cañadas wrote:
>      > HI,
>      >
>      > we talked recently about the issue of mean and expected cluster
>     size. My
>      > question now is, given that MRDS is supposed to NOT deal with a
>      > regression line to get an expected cluster size (as is one of the
>      > options in MCDS), BUT in the results section it provides the mean
>      > cluster size and the "expected" cluster size (which is actually
>      > different from that estimated front he regression line in MCDS). ANd
>      > double checking the abundance estimates, these are calculated
>     using this
>      > "expected" cluster size in MRDS, not the mean.
>      > So my question is, if it is not through a regression line, what
>     is the
>      > "expected" cluster size shown in the results section of MRDS?
>      >
>      > Many thanks
>      > Ana Canadas
