Lisa

>From a practical point of view, I’m not sure there is additional value in ESW if you have and estimate of P_a. The route to estimating density or abundance can go either through ESW or P_a. I can only guess you wish to have a value of ESW for comparison with other studies (?). It is not obvious that ESW is informative in the presence of left truncation.

The messy bit is what to do with the bit that gets truncated. Section 5.3.2 of Buckland et al. (2001) provides two choices: “relocating the line to left truncation distance (essentially subtracting left trunc distance from all distances) or by fitting the detection function only to data beyond that distance and extrapolating [the detection function] back to distance zero.”

Mathematically ESW also goes by the symbol mu () defined in Eqn 5.14 of Buckland et al. (2015) as the integral of the detection function:

with the lower bound of the integral dependent upon which course of action you took from the previous paragraph (either 0 or left trunc).

Not a very useful answer I’m afraid.

